import crate::ParseError
import crate::parse::parser::scan_run::scan_run
import redox_ast::Block

-- | Report `RDX0044` for a `return` inside a draw arrow's tail — doc 03 §7.6,
-- | plan 30.
-- |
-- | ```text
-- | conn <- pool.with_connection      pool.with_connection(|conn| {
-- | return Err Error::Bad        →        return Err(Error::Bad);   ← leaves
-- | Ok ()                                 Ok(())                      the
-- |                               })                                  closure
-- | ```
-- |
-- | That is **valid Rust**, and `with_connection` receives an `Err` where the
-- | author meant the function to return one. §7.6 calls it a *"disclosed trap,
-- | rejected rather than documented away"*, and it is worse than the usual
-- | silent lowering because the author's model — *`return` returns from the
-- | function* — is true everywhere else in the language.
-- |
-- | # `?` is deliberately untouched
-- |
-- | §7.6: *"`?` is unaffected, because it propagates out of the closure, which
-- | is the intended behaviour and is why the example above type-checks."* The
-- | two look alike and differ in exactly the way that matters, so a check
-- | rejecting both would break the one idiom the construct exists for.
-- |
-- | # Where the walk stops
-- |
-- | Into every block the tail contains, because all of those are statements in
-- | the same closure. **Not** into a lambda, a `try` block, or a nested draw
-- | arrow's tail — three exclusions that are one exclusion: another closure
-- | began, and a `return` inside it returns from that one, which is what the
-- | author wrote. A nested arrow's tail gets its own check from the recursion
-- | below, reported against the closure the `return` would actually leave.
export(crate) return_in_tail : &Block -> Vec<ParseError>
return_in_tail block =
  mut found = Vec::new ()
  scan_run (&block.stmts) false (&mut found)
  found
